1
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:46,000
When the cheering stopped, there were... Eight Men Out.

2
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:26,000
Eight Men Out is a drama, history film directed by John Sayles.

3
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Buck Weaver and Hap Felsch are young idealistic players on the Chicago White Sox, a pennant-winning team owned by Charles
Comiskey -  a penny-pinching, hands-on manager who underpays his players and treats them with disdain.

4
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:46,000
And when gamblers and hustlers discover that Comiskey's demoralized players are ripe for a
money-making scheme, one by one the team members agree to throw the World Series.

5
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:26,000
But when the White Sox are defeated, a couple of sports writers smell a fix and a
national scandal explodes, ripping the cover off America's favorite pastime.

6
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Buck Weaver, played by John Cusack.

7
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:46,000
Charles Comiskey, played by Clifton James.

8
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:26,000
Arnold Rothstein, played by Michael Lerner.

9
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
Bill Burns, played by Christopher Lloyd.

10
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:46,000
Kid Gleason, played by John Mahoney.

11
01:13:20,000 --> 01:13:26,000
Hap Felsch, played by Charlie Sheen.

12
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
Eight Men Out is a 1988 American period sports drama film based on Eliot Asinof's
1963 book Eight Men Out: The Black Sox and the 1919 World Series.

13
01:26:40,000 --> 01:26:46,000
It was written and directed by John Sayles.

14
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:26,000
The film is a dramatization of Major League Baseball's Black Sox Scandal in which eight members
of the Chicago White Sox conspired with gamblers to lose the 1919 World Series.

15
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:06,000
Most of the movie was filmed at Bush Stadium in Indianapolis, Indiana.

16
01:46:40,000 --> 01:46:46,000
The film explores themes of sports, baseball, historical figure.

17
01:53:20,000 --> 01:53:26,000
The tone is emotional.
